<p>In its 27 operating system releases, Apple <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/09/macos-27-golden-gate-review-bridging-the-tahoe-gap/">added loads of generative image editing tools</a>, and the tools have incrementally changed since they were introduced over the summer. I’m going to take my 20-plus years of experience in visual effects and talk about how well those tools work in practice.</p>
<p>I’m not going to get into a whole preamble about how <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2024/10/you-can-use-clean-up-with-a-clear-conscience/">I really do think it’s OK for you to edit a photo</a>, or <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2024/11/how-does-clean-up-measure-up/">the video comparing the initial release of Clean Up with comparable retouching tools</a>. People can do bad things with image editing tools, but I don’t believe that photos people take of their lives should be treated like courtroom evidence.</p>
<p>Having said that, it’s important that tools set users up for success by guiding them toward making good choices that preserve as much of their original images, and their original intent, as possible. Data loss of original pixels can be a trade-off to get an adjustment that people want, but tools shouldn’t hide the data loss from users.</p>
<p>Lesson number one in VFX is that you’re only changing the pixels you have to change for the effect the client wants. You do not de-noise (or de-grain) your entire image (or image sequence) to do your work and then send that back to the client. You do not re-noise the entire de-noised image either; if it can be helped, you control the region you are editing in your image to confine your work. As much of the original image as possible should pass through.</p>
<figure class="pull-right"><img height="154" sizes="(max-width: 750px) calc(100vw - 60px), (max-width: 1010px) 45vw, 345px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/show-photo-without-edits-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/show-photo-without-edits-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/show-photo-without-edits-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/show-photo-without-edits-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/show-photo-without-edits-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="394" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/show-photo-without-edits-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Screenshot of two dark buttons in a photo editor: a circular before-and-after compare icon and a pill-shaped &quot;Revert to Original&quot; button. A tooltip below reads &quot;Show photo without edits.&quot;" /><figcaption>Click the button on the left to toggle back and forth between the edited and unedited view.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Apple doesn’t always disclose to the user how its generative tools are impacting the images. That includes things like maximum resolution caps, filtering the pixels that are being kept, blurry boundaries, and potential color and value shifts. There is the Show Photo Without Edits button, which Apple provides to let people see before and after a generative edit has been made. I strongly encourage everyone to use that button. But it won’t tell you about lost resolution, and when the image is scaled to fit to the window, you may not be able to see the damage.</p>
<p>It’s totally fine if you’re happy with the zoomed-out version, and it suits the purpose (a lock screen image, a desktop, whatever). For a lot of people, <a href="https://knowyourmeme.com/memes/theyre-the-same-picture">they’re the same picture</a>. Just know that these tools don’t really match your original sensor noise, and as of the 27.2 release the images are now watermarked with <a href="https://deepmind.google/models/synthid/">SynthID</a>, even though Apple does not disclose that to users in the interface, so they can’t decide if the change they’re making is worth the image being “AI” since SynthID doesn’t disclose the editing chain.<sup id="fnref-42431-provenance"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/casting-an-expert-eye-on-apples-ai-photo-retouching-features/#fn-42431-provenance" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">1</a></sup></p>

<h2>Pretend extend doesn’t just append</h2>
<p>Cutting off the edges of a photo, and adding on to the edges of a photo, are unsurprisingly related. So much so that Apple reused its aspect-ratio crop selection code for the interface of its Extend tool. (This should be in the Crop interface.)</p>
<p>At the very least, Apple should change the code it’s reusing from the aspect ratio bounds resizing so that it <em>fits</em> to the bounds of the input as a minimum dimension when toggling between 3:4 and 9:16. Because right now, changing the aspect ratio in Extend can <em>crop</em> the image, which is what you want for Crop&#8230; but not for Extend.</p>
<figure id="video"><div style="width: 1600px;" class="wp-video"><video class="wp-video-shortcode" id="video-42431-1" width="1600" height="960" poster="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-aspect-bounds-272-poster-6c.jpeg" loop autoplay preload="auto" controls="controls"><source type="video/mp4" src="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-aspect-bounds-272-6c.mp4?_=1" /><a href="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-aspect-bounds-272-6c.mp4">https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-aspect-bounds-272-6c.mp4</a></video></div><figcaption>Cycling the aspect ratios in Extend. The selection shrinks inside the photo rather than pushing past it.</figcaption></figure>
<p>I don’t like the hazy blur effect at the edges, because users should be able to manipulate a single edge of their image without messing with pixels all the way around. There’s no useful way to snap to fit the original bounds in order to use as much original image as possible. You just have to kind of eyeball where you’re dragging the reticle.</p>
<figure id="video"><div style="width: 1600px;" class="wp-video"><video class="wp-video-shortcode" id="video-42431-2" width="1600" height="960" poster="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-48mp-softness-poster-6c.jpeg" loop autoplay preload="auto" controls="controls"><source type="video/mp4" src="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-48mp-softness-6c.mp4?_=2" /><a href="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-48mp-softness-6c.mp4">https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-48mp-softness-6c.mp4</a></video></div><figcaption>Changing the aspect ratio in Extend, and the haze landing on all four edges whether or not you are pushing them out. The border around the entire image softens to blend with the generative extend features. The core of the image softens from being scaled down.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The unfortunate truth is that Extend throws away image data. It only <em>looks</em> like it keeps your original pixels when you’re zoomed way out. It perceptually reads like you’ve extended your image, but what’s really happened is that the original image is actually scaled down and resampled to be a smaller resolution so that your generative border, plus your original pixels, all fit in a 12.19-megapixel canvas.</p>
<p>It appears that Apple is using a soft filter to resample the image—not even a sharpening filter, because everything is definitely softer and there are no ringing artifacts associated with sharpening.</p>
<p>The blurry border from the crop preview is also true to what’s happening, because the entire border <em>is</em> blurry. There seems to be about 200-300 pixels of a feathered mask operation that takes your scaled-down photo and combines it with the generative edge.</p>
<figure><img height="2060" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-duck-softness-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-duck-softness-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-duck-softness-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-duck-softness-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-duck-softness-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-duck-softness-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Comparison titled &quot;Extend softens the pixels it keeps&quot;: a mallard duck photo at original 6048×8064 versus Extend 3024×4032, with detail crops of the duck's head showing the Extend version looking blurrier and softer than the original." /><figcaption>This 48 megapixel image is extra soft because it hits the resolution cap for Extend and the entire image is scaled down even further. Feathers and specular highlights all smear even though they have nothing to do with the edge of frame.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><img height="830" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-expectation-reality-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-expectation-reality-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-expectation-reality-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-expectation-reality-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-expectation-reality-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-expectation-reality-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Diagram titled &quot;Expectation vs. Reality&quot; showing a duck on blue water. Users think generative expand enlarges the image; actually the original (3024×4032) is scaled down to 3023×4033, with generated pixels filling the bounds, capped at 12.19 megapixels." /><figcaption>Scaled to show relative alignment when extending equally to the maximum value along all borders. The final image is nearly the same resolution as the original.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><img height="800" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-original-scale-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-original-scale-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-original-scale-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-original-scale-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-original-scale-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/extend-original-scale-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Chart titled &quot;Where your original ends up in Extend&quot; comparing three photos (Coit Tower, duck on water, ducks on land) with source, Extend output, and scaled-original sizes. The original keeps 80%, 44%, and 16% of its pixel count respectively." /><figcaption>Some diagrams showing the bounds differences and the impact on the original resolution.</figcaption></figure>
<p><em>Imagine</em> that I said that I wanted to add a room to a one-story house that had no property boundary constraints, and the contractor did it by demolishing the house and building a scaled-down replica of the house, then adding the extra room on so the whole house took the same square footage. That’s some Nathan Fielder behavior.</p>
<p>If the generative maximum is 12.19 MP, or something around that, why not take those generative pixels and scale those <em>up</em> to match the increased bounds so that the original core can be preserved? Don’t blend any borders that aren’t actually being extended! Forcing everything to fit some technical constraint rather than making preservation of data the constraint is backwards.</p>
<p>Another place where data could be pulled from to improve quality is other neighboring photos you’ve taken. Extend doesn’t use other photos in your camera roll from the same time frame, or location, as context. You can easily see this for yourself if you duplicate a photo, crop the photo, and then use Extend on the crop, you won’t get buildings sourced from the neighboring image. It’s a silly test, but it just confirms it’s not happening.</p>
<p>Context is very important to getting good results, because it’s what a real photo retoucher would do, or a VFX artist. It’s one thing if you don’t have the data, in which case filling in what was <em>probably</em> there is much more impressive, but if you could have filled in what you have photos of, then you should do that.</p>
<p>Which brings us to&#8230;</p>
<h2>Reframe to blame for eye strain</h2>
<p>The Reframe feature is the most “we repurposed something” of the models. I would argue that it is not a <em>creative</em> image editing tool at all, but it is <em>very</em> clever. If you look at Apple’s <a href="https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/sharp-monocular-view">ML Sharp</a>, or any of its other myriad projects around 3D computer vision, you can see a lineage of sorts. (This means that things like reflections and refractions do weird stuff. Pond surfaces, shop windows, wine glasses, and the like.)</p>
<p>Reframe lets you move around quite a bit, and you’ll see all the stretchy weird stuff, and holes, from areas where the camera did not have line of sight. Reframe does not use context from surrounding photos, or video, to fill in those missing pieces, unfortunately. Instead those holes are filled in with speculation from some kind of model.</p>
<p>Reframe doesn’t retain any of your original images, even scaled down, like Extend does. It’s filling in everything seemingly guided by its extrapolated 3D view that&#8217;s full of holes. It doesn’t have access to the original imagery to paste back in.</p>
<p>However, where Extend mostly softens and mushes original pixels, Reframe also has tone and color shifts that are not anchored to your original image.</p>
<figure><img height="1779" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Comparison titled &quot;Reframe repaints the whole frame, not just the gaps.&quot; Original and Reframe versions of a white egret by a rocky pond, with 100% detail crops below showing the egret's head and beak against dark rocks." /><figcaption>The virtual camera barely moves, and all hues and tones shift.</figcaption></figure>
