yellow patch near its dark bill stands in shallow, dark green water beside a rocky bank lined with grass. Ripples spread across the foreground. A caption reads &quot;Egret · Project Indigo&quot; with a &quot;Reframe&quot; button." data-label="Reframe" /><figcaption>The egret frame, for comparison, original to Reframe.</figcaption></figure>
<figure id="video"><div style="width: 1600px;" class="wp-video"><video class="wp-video-shortcode" id="video-42431-3" width="1600" height="960" poster="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-reflection-poster-6c.jpeg" loop autoplay preload="auto" controls="controls"><source type="video/mp4" src="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-reflection-6c.mp4?_=3" /><a href="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-reflection-6c.mp4">https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-egret-reflection-6c.mp4</a></video></div><figcaption>Dragging the Reframe controls. Watch the reflection rather than the bird to see a dent in the water.</figcaption></figure>
<p>These color and value shifts are not uniform, and aren’t restricted to certain file types. The SDR image from my Sony a6400 has shifts, and the egret photo was taken with Project Indigo, not the Camera app. There isn’t an entirely consistent and predictable direction of color and tone shifts. Sometimes it’s more drastic than others, or a defect might be similar with shots taken around the same time of day.</p>
<p>To take the angle adjustment out of the calculation I took a bunch of shots with gray and white to measure color cast changes and only changed the image by 0.99 zoom. This artificially small adjustment also shows how much Reframe hammers the image since all pixels need to be redrawn, but it’s much easier to compare directly to source than if I was wildly swinging the camera around. If you want to do your own tests I would recommend a tiny adjustment like this to make comparisons easier.</p>
<figure id="video"><div style="width: 1400px;" class="wp-video"><video class="wp-video-shortcode" id="video-42431-4" width="1400" height="1050" poster="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-flip-hdr-poster-6c.jpeg" loop autoplay preload="auto" controls="controls"><source type="video/mp4" src="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-flip-hdr-6c.mp4?_=4" /><a href="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-flip-hdr-6c.mp4">https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-flip-hdr-6c.mp4</a></video></div><figcaption>Cycling through 12 photos taken at the same time with large patches of gray and white to show color cast shifts by only changing the zoom setting from 1.00 to 0.99 in Reframe. All colors and values shift in addition to overall quality loss.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><img height="1566" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-color-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-color-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-color-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-color-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-color-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-color-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Chart titled &quot;Which way the color moves — twelve Reframe pairs&quot;: photos overlaid with blue and amber arrows, plus a scatter plot of 2,288 cells. Median shift ΔCt +0.0031, ΔCp -0.0009; most cells move toward blue and green." /><figcaption>Twelve photographs taken the same morning, each run through Reframe with only the zoom moved from 1.00 to 0.99. Arrows are drawn where the sample was taken and point the way that patch moved; blue arrows moved toward blue, amber toward yellow, and length is the size of the shift. Measured in BT.2100 ICtCp with diffuse white at 203 nits, which puts 2.3 stops of headroom at 1000. ICtCp is a colorspace measuring human perception of color in HDR. 1000 nits is what most high-end Apple displays sustain these days. Each file is rendered at its own declared headroom, and headroom is identical within all twelve pairs. Cells without a valid match in both frames (completely invented details occluded in the original image) are omitted.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><img height="1566" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-luma-gain-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-luma-gain-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-luma-gain-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-luma-gain-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-luma-gain-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-luma-gain-vectorfield-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Chart titled &quot;SDR level against gain map, cell by cell — twelve Reframe pairs&quot;: photo thumbnails with red and green arrows, plus a scatter plot of 2,288 cells. Median ΔY -2.85, Δgain -1.11; both fall together in 77% of cells; r = +0.18." /><figcaption>Not a color space &mdash; the horizontal axis is the change in SDR level and the vertical axis the change in the gain map. Both fall together in most (77%) of the regions. The green regions are places where the base got brighter while the gain map dropped, so the two trade against each other and the result flattens in HDR without looking wrong in SDR.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><img height="1220" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-hdr-cost-272-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-hdr-cost-272-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-hdr-cost-272-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-hdr-cost-272-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-hdr-cost-272-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-hdr-cost-272-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Infographic &quot;What Reframe costs on an HDR display&quot;: a heatmap of a street sign photo shows dimmer highlights and brighter shadows, beside a line chart where brightness drops from -0.2% on SDR to -7.2% at 2 and 3 stops of HDR." /><figcaption>Both files declare the same headroom but don’t hit the same max values, and Reframe’s diffused version shifts where some of it is allocated. Look at the difference in contrast of the screen right clouds over the bright sky, and the row of bushes. They trend in different directions from the source image. </figcaption></figure>
<p>This is technically very sophisticated, and the part where you can just drag your mouse, or finger, around to move things is pretty neat. Unfortunately, my critique of the control scheme is that people can’t quite do the kinds of recomposition that they might want to do to a photo from a 2D perspective. “Move this person to the left” has to be “Move the camera around this person” instead.</p>
<figure><img height="1290" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Comparison grid titled &quot;Reframe and the hairline&quot;: a smiling man in sunglasses before Tower Bridge, original (6000×4000) versus Reframe (4276×2851), with 100% hairline detail crops below showing the original and Reframe versions." /><figcaption>Individual strands survive in the original and combine into clumps in the Reframe.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><img height="1024" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-color-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-color-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-color-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-color-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-color-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-color-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Side-by-side photos of a smiling man in sunglasses before Tower Bridge, labeled Original and Reframe, with a table of CIELAB patch differences for sky, river water, bridge stone, road surface and lit skin showing Reframe darkens and greens." /><figcaption>The sky loses 3.1 L* and the river 3.3, while the two near-neutral surfaces &mdash; the bridge stone and the road &mdash; turn green. This one darkens and shifts color at once.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Here’s my partner Jason in front of Tower Bridge in London. I like the photo just fine, no regrets, but it’s arguable that it would have been helped if Jason was standing slightly to screen right. These controls <em>can’t</em> move Jason. If I shift the perspective until Jason’s body is no longer overlapping with Tower Bridge then Jason is no longer facing the camera, and we’re looking at him from a three-quarter view instead of front on.</p>
<p>I struggle to think of a use case for orbiting around a person unless you’re Michael Bay.</p>
<figure id="video"><div style="width: 1600px;" class="wp-video"><video class="wp-video-shortcode" id="video-42431-5" width="1600" height="960" poster="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-poster-6c.jpeg" loop autoplay preload="auto" controls="controls"><source type="video/mp4" src="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-6c.mp4?_=5" /><a href="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-6c.mp4">https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-tower-bridge-6c.mp4</a></video></div><figcaption>Pushing the Reframe controls as far as they go. Jason turns away from the camera before he clears the bridge.</figcaption></figure>
<p>In another example, where I took a selfie of Jason and me at the Eiffel Tower, I tried to use Reframe to make it look like less of a selfie (again, <em>I don’t care</em> that it looks like a selfie, but I know normal people <em>will</em> do these things). I’m not swinging wildly off axis, but more of the background is revealed from parallax, and not sourced from context. Most importantly, Reframe caught my knuckles and turned them into some kind of cotton candy cancer.</p>
<figure><img height="580" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-neighboring-photos-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-neighboring-photos-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-neighboring-photos-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-neighboring-photos-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-neighboring-photos-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-neighboring-photos-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Screenshot titled &quot;Frames Reframe does not consult&quot; with the label &quot;Neighbouring frames from the same session,&quot; showing four photos of a man in a striped sweater before the Eiffel Tower; the last, highlighted in blue, adds a second man beside him." /><figcaption>Frames shot moments apart, none of which Reframe consults.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><img height="1779" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-eiffel-knuckles-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-eiffel-knuckles-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-eiffel-knuckles-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-eiffel-knuckles-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-eiffel-knuckles-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1600" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/reframe-eiffel-knuckles-6c.jpeg?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Before-and-after comparison of a selfie of two smiling men in front of the Eiffel Tower. The original is shown beside the Reframe version, with 100% detail crops of the chin and collar revealing a knuckle added at the left edge by Reframe." /><figcaption>In the original the knuckles are a sliver at the frame edge. Reframe promotes them to a shape.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Big nope. Surely, I can use Clean Up to remove my knuckles?</p>
<h2>Clean Up re-up</h2>
<p>The new Clean Up is not what I would consider to be two years better than <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2024/11/how-does-clean-up-measure-up/">the original Clean Up</a>. It couldn’t paint my knuckles out without inserting a flesh-colored seam in the shoulder, or other streak artifacts. I don’t understand why this is quite so bad at simple requests.</p>
<p>But it turns out that there are two Clean Ups: when the Image Quality is set to Fast and when it&#8217;s set to High Quality. (There&#8217;s also an &#8220;Auto&#8221; setting that usually gave me Fast results.) The High Quality pass involves using a server-based model, so it&#8217;s slower&#8230; but it&#8217;s also much better.</p>
<p>A flaw in <em>both</em> Clean Ups is that there’s image segmentation that tries to intelligently only select the hand I want to remove, which always leaves a few pink pixels where the hand pixels are at their thinnest just as they disappear over the shoulder edge. I had to hit that with another round of paint. This isn’t a model limitation, like when Fast Clean Up added pink pixels; this is a selection issue.</p>
<figure id="video"><div style="width: 1440px;" class="wp-video"><video class="wp-video-shortcode" id="video-42431-6" width="1440" height="864" poster="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cleanup-reframe-combo-poster-6c.jpeg" loop autoplay preload="auto" controls="controls"><source type="video/mp4" src="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cleanup-reframe-combo-6c.mp4?_=6" /><a href="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cleanup-reframe-combo-6c.mp4">https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cleanup-reframe-combo-6c.mp4</a></video></div><figcaption>Clean Up on High Quality taking the knuckles out, then Reframe on the result.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The good news is that Clean Up is the only generative tool in Photos that doesn’t alter the <em>entire</em> image. It does seem to dilate out from the paint operation and blend back to the photo, so the trees above my hand shift, but that’s more in line with what I would expect from this tool than what Extend and Reframe do.</p>
<p>It would be nice if I could more precisely paint where I wanted it to work, not where I wanted it to selectively decide to work, though.</p>
<h2>Crop shop image plops</h2>
<p>The concerns I have about unnecessary modification to original pixels are not the kinds of things that everyone in the general public would notice or object to. It might look a little shifted, but they just wanted this for some lock screen, photo frame, or highly compressed Instagram carousel. I do think it’s up to Apple to do its best since there is no technical reason for Apple not to.</p>
<p>The real obstacle for most people will be tool use.</p>
<p>Providing these individualized models to people that have some overlapping function exposes the models Apple has ready to ship, not the workflow it sees people using. (The most basic example being the need to provide a one-stop shop for cropping and composition tools.)</p>
<p>I would also remind Apple that it doesn’t ship a competitive set of tools for boring, deterministic, 2D operations like straightening, perspective operations, and subject-aware cropping. How many times have we collectively clicked the “auto” button in the Crop panel and it didn’t do what we wanted?</p>
<p>Why are we fighting with center-weighted vertical and horizontal perspective tilt operations instead of having an automated perspective adjustment like Lightroom? Instead, we have an assortment of nondeterministic image diffusion models running on servers, which is way more complicated. We can’t just hit a button to fix some skewing lines with a simple 2D operation? Something that doesn’t put the scarlet letters “AI” on the image?</p>
<figure id="video"><div style="width: 1800px;" class="wp-video"><video class="wp-video-shortcode" id="video-42431-7" width="1800" height="926" poster="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/lightroom-vs-reframe-poster-6c.jpeg" loop autoplay preload="auto" controls="controls"><source type="video/mp4" src="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/lightroom-vs-reframe-6c.mp4?_=7" /><a href="https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/lightroom-vs-reframe-6c.mp4">https://sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/lightroom-vs-reframe-6c.mp4</a></video></div><figcaption>Lightroom&#8217;s automatic perspective correction on the left, Crop and Reframe on the right, on the same photo.</figcaption></figure>
<p>I get it that generative models sound fancy, but this is for a public that is not going to scrutinize the tools as much as image-editing nerds are. The tools aren&#8217;t good enough just because normal people can’t tell how much of their original image was destroyed. I know that Apple wants these tools to seem magical, but they’re all still kind of just demos that have escaped containment. I&#8217;m not sure they solve as many real-world problems as Apple would like them to.</p>
